{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789341860,"nonce":"36nVg9gBJ5GMBFH13Sqop1I1fs8=","title":"Popping a caller-armed trap looks the exception state up again","message":"`hlp_setup_trap_in` (0d1d0a0) arms a trap in the caller's storage under one\nthread-local access to the exception state; `hlp_remove_trap_jit` after the\ncall takes a second one, plus the RefCell borrow, to find the trap it is\npopping, which the caller already has in hand. On macOS each access is an\nout-of-line `_tlv_get_addr`. Ash's sampler on caribou's Wren-to-Haxe static\ncall put the trap at about 12 ns of a 47 ns call, of which the remove's\nlookup was around 2.\n\nFix: the context records the exception state it was chained into when it\nis armed (`TrapContext::state`, null for a pooled context), and\n`hlp_remove_trap_in(storage)` unlinks it through that, falling back to\n`remove_trap` if the storage is not the innermost trap. The setup keeps its\none lookup and the `gc_lock_held_depth` read the throw path restores from.","files":null}]}